{"candidates":[{"claim":"Resumption may proceed if and only if the event framing protocol provides deterministic delimiters that allow trailing partial bytes to be classified as non-events; resumption must be refused when framing is ambiguous and cannot distinguish a complete event from a partial fragment.","counterconditions":["If the framing protocol relies solely on external length-prefixing without end-markers or checksums, trailing bytes cannot be deterministically classified as non-events, requiring refusal of resumption.","If the resumption protocol attempts to garbage-collect or rewrite the partial bytes rather than ignoring them, it modifies the append-only log and must be refused.","If no complete event was framed before exhaustion, resumption cannot anchor causal continuity and must be refused until a complete event is available."],"mechanism":"A partial byte sequence left by mid-stream exhaustion lacks event identity under proper framing. The resumption protocol simply ignores trailing bytes after the last framed complete event. No append-only violation occurs because no committed event was deleted or modified—non-events were skipped, preserving both physical append-only integrity and logical causal continuity.","optional_refs":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","d1c3422e3d18993ac034bc5bbd3b90f90d3294847aca130493c4c8c6272330cb"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["ba687c5f145e404c53a84549cf366e0b6e0106eec67404c07231357fed09e672","d1c3422e3d18993ac034bc5bbd3b90f90d3294847aca130493c4c8c6272330cb"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.75}],"simulation_proposals":[]}